# RFC2180 section 3 lets us refuse RENAME of a mailbox another session
# has selected. We don't, and haven't since 2008: the mailbox keeps its
# id, its uidvalidity and its UIDs, so both sessions carry on under the
# new name.
#
# Renaming inbox leaves a new, empty inbox behind. That is a different
# mailbox holding the same name, and the UIDs that used to live under
# the name went with the mailbox we moved away, so it gets a higher
# uidvalidity: RFC3501 2.3.1.1 requires one when the UIDs do not
# persist. It inherits uidnext as well, so that a client which cached
# inbox and never noticed the rename cannot be handed a UID it has
# already seen attached to a different message.
depends 2180base
send 1
a rename inbox x
receive 1
a OK done
send 1
b uid fetch 1:* (uid)
receive 5
* 1 FETCH (UID 1)
* 2 FETCH (UID 2)
* 3 FETCH (UID 3)
* 4 FETCH (UID 4)
b OK done
send 1
c status x (messages uidnext uidvalidity)
receive 2
* STATUS x (MESSAGES 4 UIDNEXT 5 UIDVALIDITY 1)
c OK done
send 1
d status inbox (messages uidnext uidvalidity)
receive 2
* STATUS INBOX (MESSAGES 0 UIDNEXT 5 UIDVALIDITY 2)
d OK done
use watcher
send 1
e uid fetch 1:* (uid)
receive 5
* 1 FETCH (UID 1)
* 2 FETCH (UID 2)
* 3 FETCH (UID 3)
* 4 FETCH (UID 4)
e OK done
end
